{
  // Why this file exists — the same reason `src/utils/events/tsconfig.json` does, and this
  // directory needing its own copy is the whole point of it.
  //
  // This directory is compiled by two projects: Astro for the browser, and `tsc` into the Cloud
  // Functions bundle (functions/tsconfig.json includes it by path rather than keeping a copy, so
  // the app and the collector cannot disagree about what touches a route).
  //
  // Vite resolves a tsconfig by walking UP from the file it is transforming. Without this file that
  // walk reaches korczak-xyz/tsconfig.json, which `extends: "astro/tsconfigs/strict"` — resolvable
  // only where the site's node_modules are installed. On the CI runner that installs only the
  // functions' dependencies it is not, and `functions/src/transit/*.test.ts` died with
  // `Failed to load tsconfig 'astro/tsconfigs/strict': Tsconfig not found`, naming a file that has
  // nothing wrong with it. It cost a deploy: the Metro Watch commit's own rules never reached
  // Firestore, so the app came up reading a corpus it had no permission to see and said
  // `Missing or insufficient permissions.` The events directory only escaped because the functions
  // import types from it at runtime, and a type import is erased before anything is transformed.
  //
  // Nothing here may need an Astro or React setting: that is the same constraint portable.test.ts
  // enforces on the imports, stated once more in the shape the compiler reads.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2022",
    "lib": ["es2022"],
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": false
  },
  "include": ["*.ts"]
}
